(465539, 42719, 1368, '<td valign="top">I love this picture Should have gotten a ribbon. Beautiful!</td>', 'I love this picture Should have gotten a ribbon. Beautiful!', '2003-10-29 08:18:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(465701, 43017, 5475, '<td valign="top">Hi Mom!  i think you did a great job on this shot.  the composition is perfect, and the lighting effect is very well done.  I have noticed in a couple of photos of yours in dark conditions that you get a yellow cast...  I don''t know if you camera has a white balance adjustment, but you may want to check into that.  I think your teddy bear photo has some yellow cast to it as well.  <br/>All in all, you did a great job, but the image has some camera limitations affecting it (white balance and grain or noise in dark images).  I like pcody''s idea to shoot this with the light reflection making a halo at her head!  Well done!<br/>Love,<br/>Andy<br/> <br/>(edit)  I just noticed that your ISO was 320 for this shot.  Try to set it lower if your camera will let you and it will minimize the noise/grain.  It will also require a longer shutter speed, but you were already at 1/2 second, so I assume you used a tripod or other stable surface for the camera.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-10-29 10:14:55.</i></td>', 'Hi Mom!  i think you did a great job on this shot.  the composition is perfect, and the lighting effect is very well done.  I have noticed in a couple of photos of yours in dark conditions that you get a yellow cast...  I don''t know if you camera has a white balance adjustment, but you may want to check into that.  I think your teddy bear photo has some yellow cast to it as well.  All in all, you did a great job, but the image has some camera limitations affecting it (white balance and grain or noise in dark images).  I like pcody''s idea to shoot this with the light reflection making a halo at her head!  Well done!Love,Andy (edit)  I just noticed that your ISO was 320 for this shot.  Try to set it lower if your camera will let you and it will minimize the noise/grain.  It will also require a longer shutter speed, but you were already at 1/2 second, so I assume you used a tripod or other stable surface for the camera.', '2003-10-29 10:12:51.000000', '2003-10-29 10:14:55.000000', 0),
(465762, 42884, 11693, '<td valign="top">I still think your shot should have been in the top 3.  Fourth is good though.  I would respectfully ask your opinion of my # 29 First Light Photo.  It is my best place yet.  You have a good eye and your opinion would be most valuable.<br/> <br/>Thanks,<br/> <br/>David</td>', 'I still think your shot should have been in the top 3.  Fourth is good though.  I would respectfully ask your opinion of my # 29 First Light Photo.  It is my best place yet.  You have a good eye and your opinion would be most valuable. Thanks, David', '2003-10-29 11:04:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(465992, 42325, 8860, '<td valign="top">I think this shot has been harshly judged. It''s got some lovely, smooth tones and an intriguing composition. Its size is not a major benefit but it''s still pretty.<br/> <br/>Composition could benefit from being more off-centre.</td>', 'I think this shot has been harshly judged. It''s got some lovely, smooth tones and an intriguing composition. Its size is not a major benefit but it''s still pretty. Composition could benefit from being more off-centre.', '2003-10-29 13:08:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(466252, 42685, 7889, '<td valign="top">ah...the cabaret dancer man......</td>', 'ah...the cabaret dancer man......', '2003-10-29 16:10:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(466341, 42434, 3290, '<td valign="top">Hi David,<br/> <br/>I like this shot - I would pretty much agree with timj351''s comments. The upper part of the picture is stunning. The colours and three-dimensional feel to the sky are very nice. The dark area at the bottom is too large - you could reduce the size of it by 1/2 or 2/3rds and I think that would improve it. One other point - the eye seems to like to have a focal point in a shot like this; something for it to rest on - a tree or person in silhouette, for example. You might try a similar shot (if you get another lovely sunrise like this) with such an element included. I hope these comments are helpful.</td>', 'Hi David, I like this shot - I would pretty much agree with timj351''s comments. The upper part of the picture is stunning. The colours and three-dimensional feel to the sky are very nice. The dark area at the bottom is too large - you could reduce the size of it by 1/2 or 2/3rds and I think that would improve it. One other point - the eye seems to like to have a focal point in a shot like this; something for it to rest on - a tree or person in silhouette, for example. You might try a similar shot (if you get another lovely sunrise like this) with such an element included. I hope these comments are helpful.', '2003-10-29 17:30:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(466943, 43009, 6202, '<td valign="top">"Critique Club"<br/> <br/>I find this a very inspiring photo. But it has a lot of flaws at the same time.  While the composition itself is good the focus appears slightly off and noticable more in the wording than anywhere else.  Second thing that in some ways adds to the feeling of the photo is the jet overhead.  As much as it adds to the photo it also takes from the photo, and creates a distraction it also appears to be over edited and that is noticable in that jet stream, as it isn''t a solid fluid line it is broken up.<br/> <br/>I do think you met the challenge well with lighting, it is a nice image with only a few minor flaws.</td>', '"Critique Club" I find this a very inspiring photo. But it has a lot of flaws at the same time.  While the composition itself is good the focus appears slightly off and noticable more in the wording than anywhere else.  Second thing that in some ways adds to the feeling of the photo is the jet overhead.  As much as it adds to the photo it also takes from the photo, and creates a distraction it also appears to be over edited and that is noticable in that jet stream, as it isn''t a solid fluid line it is broken up. I do think you met the challenge well with lighting, it is a nice image with only a few minor flaws.', '2003-10-30 06:49:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(466950, 42994, 6202, '<td valign="top">"Critique Club"<br/> <br/>Nice image.  The colors are good as are the details.  Techinally the photo is good, however as good as it is techincally it lacks the "personality" it hold your attention.  The orange leaves grab your attention but once it has your attention it doesn''t hold it cause nothing else in the photos grabs you, that tree overwelms the photo and distracts the view from the cloud leaving it almost as if the photo isn''t balanced out.<br/> <br/>I do feel you met the challenge, and again I will say your colors are really good as are the details.</td>', '"Critique Club" Nice image.  The colors are good as are the details.  Techinally the photo is good, however as good as it is techincally it lacks the "personality" it hold your attention.  The orange leaves grab your attention but once it has your attention it doesn''t hold it cause nothing else in the photos grabs you, that tree overwelms the photo and distracts the view from the cloud leaving it almost as if the photo isn''t balanced out. I do feel you met the challenge, and again I will say your colors are really good as are the details.', '2003-10-30 06:57:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(467027, 42902, 13218, '<td valign="top">Great stuff. wonderful lighting. well done. Love it. bravo.</td>', 'Great stuff. wonderful lighting. well done. Love it. bravo.', '2003-10-30 09:16:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(467315, 42693, 6943, '<td valign="top">Love the tones/colors in this photo, really sets a mood. Gave it a 10 myself...nice shot.</td>', 'Love the tones/colors in this photo, really sets a mood. Gave it a 10 myself...nice shot.', '2003-10-30 13:22:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(467432, 42916, 6999, '<td valign="top">Right, i''ve deliberately waited until later than usual to write my critiques, to see if it changes anything, and of course it doesn''t. <br/> <br/>I like the mood of this one Anna - even though the focus seems to have ''missed'', and the rocks behind the child seem more in focus than he is. Plenty og good and useful comments too, which is good to see.<br/> <br/>I''d like to see what he''s doing with the stick, even if it''s just waving around in mid-air, especially as the composition really pulls the focus that way - the movement of the eye from falls, to child, to stick is very natural and it''s a touch a shame that the end of the stick isn''t there. I think focussing fully on the child, and leaving the bacground to blur out with depth of field would add impact, but perhaps would lose some of the tones of the rocks. The selecive colour thing works, as some have said, to bring the eye to the subject, and works well for that: and it''s a trick I mostly don''t like at all.<br/> <br/>One of your better shots, I think Anna. <br/> <br/>ed</td>', 'Right, i''ve deliberately waited until later than usual to write my critiques, to see if it changes anything, and of course it doesn''t.  I like the mood of this one Anna - even though the focus seems to have ''missed'', and the rocks behind the child seem more in focus than he is. Plenty og good and useful comments too, which is good to see. I''d like to see what he''s doing with the stick, even if it''s just waving around in mid-air, especially as the composition really pulls the focus that way - the movement of the eye from falls, to child, to stick is very natural and it''s a touch a shame that the end of the stick isn''t there. I think focussing fully on the child, and leaving the bacground to blur out with depth of field would add impact, but perhaps would lose some of the tones of the rocks. The selecive colour thing works, as some have said, to bring the eye to the subject, and works well for that: and it''s a trick I mostly don''t like at all. One of your better shots, I think Anna.  ed', '2003-10-30 15:32:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(467443, 42815, 6999, '<td valign="top">Hi from the critique club Jon - doing my bit for rationality etc.<br/> <br/>Gave this a 6, which is absolutely my average mark these days, and I don''t know that I''d change that. Your own comments I think are spot-on, though I''m not completely convinced about the challenge: many many people took shots that I think would more appropriately have been for a challenge called ''Light'' rather than ''Lighting'', and i was hoping for more people to use light to shape and define their subjects, rather than go for pretty patterns: there are those here who have the ability, and few did. So within those terms, I didn''t think this was out of the ordinary, but within the terms imposed by what was actually entered, it was a little above ordinary - so placing 26th is pretty good.<br/> <br/>The colours seem a touch cast - like they''ve been pushed toward the incandescent spectrum, rather than a ''true'' centred spectrum: if the light coming directly through the bottles above the liquids was more ''white'' i think the colours would jump more, though perhaps the feeling of a dark bar and a glow from behind the bottles would be lost. It does transmit something of that feeling, of those nights when the barman just seems to be a silhouette, and you can''t quite amke out the labels on the bottles, but perhps needs a figure to really do that; as it is, as you say, it''s just patterns, and no particular focus.<br/> <br/>Ed</td>', 'Hi from the critique club Jon - doing my bit for rationality etc. Gave this a 6, which is absolutely my average mark these days, and I don''t know that I''d change that. Your own comments I think are spot-on, though I''m not completely convinced about the challenge: many many people took shots that I think would more appropriately have been for a challenge called ''Light'' rather than ''Lighting'', and i was hoping for more people to use light to shape and define their subjects, rather than go for pretty patterns: there are those here who have the ability, and few did. So within those terms, I didn''t think this was out of the ordinary, but within the terms imposed by what was actually entered, it was a little above ordinary - so placing 26th is pretty good. The colours seem a touch cast - like they''ve been pushed toward the incandescent spectrum, rather than a ''true'' centred spectrum: if the light coming directly through the bottles above the liquids was more ''white'' i think the colours would jump more, though perhaps the feeling of a dark bar and a glow from behind the bottles would be lost. It does transmit something of that feeling, of those nights when the barman just seems to be a silhouette, and you can''t quite amke out the labels on the bottles, but perhps needs a figure to really do that; as it is, as you say, it''s just patterns, and no particular focus. Ed', '2003-10-30 15:46:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(467459, 42816, 6999, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club Dave.<br/> <br/>Last?! Well, i think that''s underserved, certainly. Not a winner, but hey. <br/> <br/>The idea''s fine - in fact perhaps one of the better ideas for this challenge, but the execution is certainly what''s brought your score so low. I have no idea how possible it is to get a clean, un-noisey image of a camera below such a bright flash, without resorting to double-exposures and the like, but had you been able to do that the score would be a load higher. Also, something to black out the background around the Minolta would have helped, those lines of tthe door and window(?) look like an accident. <br/> <br/>Ed</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club Dave. Last?! Well, i think that''s underserved, certainly. Not a winner, but hey.  The idea''s fine - in fact perhaps one of the better ideas for this challenge, but the execution is certainly what''s brought your score so low. I have no idea how possible it is to get a clean, un-noisey image of a camera below such a bright flash, without resorting to double-exposures and the like, but had you been able to do that the score would be a load higher. Also, something to black out the background around the Minolta would have helped, those lines of tthe door and window(?) look like an accident.  Ed', '2003-10-30 16:01:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(467709, 42693, 10985, '<td valign="top">The light and angle give this a very powerful impact.</td>', 'The light and angle give this a very powerful impact.', '2003-10-30 21:11:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(468005, 42087, 5728, '<td valign="top">Le Club Critique, bonjour Renee.<br/> <br/>Well I''ve decided to double my critiques for the CC this month to "2". You are number two. When I first saw this pic I thought it was a wooden post sticking out of something concrete, the texture of your apple does give me the impression of concrete somehow. I eventually figured out what it is though. I like photos where I have to spend a bit longer looking at it to find out what it is, or how it is done etc.<br/> <br/>The B&amp;W and the play of shadows on the surface of the apple combine well together. The cropping is a little unusual. Actually Kavey hit the nail on the head with her comments below, I can''t really add to what she has already written. <br/> <br/>I only wish you had included the exposure details with this image in the "Photographic Information" tab. Other than that, well done, it''s a good photo.</td>', 'Le Club Critique, bonjour Renee. Well I''ve decided to double my critiques for the CC this month to "2". You are number two. When I first saw this pic I thought it was a wooden post sticking out of something concrete, the texture of your apple does give me the impression of concrete somehow. I eventually figured out what it is though. I like photos where I have to spend a bit longer looking at it to find out what it is, or how it is done etc. The B&W and the play of shadows on the surface of the apple combine well together. The cropping is a little unusual. Actually Kavey hit the nail on the head with her comments below, I can''t really add to what she has already written.  I only wish you had included the exposure details with this image in the "Photographic Information" tab. Other than that, well done, it''s a good photo.', '2003-10-31 09:11:24.000000', NULL, 0),
